1
00:00:00,238 --> 00:00:05,572
You type a URL, hit enter, and a Next.js page shows up almost instantly.

2
00:00:05,966 --> 00:00:09,060
But between your click and that page, a lot is happening.

3
00:00:10,350 --> 00:00:14,468
In the next 10 minutes, I'll draw out exactly how Next.js works.

4
00:00:14,862 --> 00:00:22,116
From the folders in your project, to the server, to the HTML, to the moment the page becomes interactive in your browser.

5
00:00:23,438 --> 00:00:29,764
By the end, you'll be able to explain it clearly in an interview, and you'll know why your app is fast or why it's slow.

6
00:00:30,158 --> 00:00:31,204
Here's the plan.

7
00:00:33,070 --> 00:00:35,428
Here's exactly what you'll learn in this video.

8
00:00:35,758 --> 00:00:39,520
First, what Next.js actually adds on top of React.

9
00:00:39,760 --> 00:00:42,480
Then, how routing works with plain folders.

10
00:00:42,798 --> 00:00:46,564
Then the big one, server components versus client components.

11
00:00:47,854 --> 00:00:52,720
Next, we'll follow a single request, from the proxy all the way to hydration.

12
00:00:53,134 --> 00:01:00,420
We'll see how streaming with suspense works and how NextJS decides what's static, what's dynamic, and what gets cached.

13
00:01:01,678 --> 00:01:08,708
After that, navigation without page reloads, server actions for changing data, and how build and deploy work.

14
00:01:09,102 --> 00:01:12,804
And we'll finish with three interview questions so you can test yourself.

15
00:01:14,126 --> 00:01:16,612
So let's start with the most basic question.

16
00:01:18,542 --> 00:01:20,400
First, what even is Next.js?

17
00:01:20,974 --> 00:01:25,060
React by itself is just a library for building user interfaces.

18
00:01:25,486 --> 00:01:31,748
It doesn't decide how pages are routed, how data is loaded, or how your code gets to the browser.

19
00:01:33,038 --> 00:01:42,020
In a classic React single-page app, the server sends an almost empty HTML file with one div and a big JavaScript bundle.

20
00:01:42,510 --> 00:01:47,076
The user stares at a blank screen until that JavaScript downloads and runs.

21
00:01:48,366 --> 00:01:51,600
Next.js is a framework built on top of React.

22
00:01:51,886 --> 00:01:57,828
It adds a router, a server that renders your components, a bundler called Turbopack, and a compiler.

23
00:01:58,318 --> 00:02:01,476
All wired together so you don't have to set them up yourself.

24
00:02:02,766 --> 00:02:04,320
The big idea is simple.

25
00:02:04,622 --> 00:02:09,380
Render on the server first so the user gets real HTML right away.

26
00:02:09,806 --> 00:02:13,316
Then let React take over in the browser to make it interactive.

27
00:02:15,182 --> 00:02:16,320
Now, routing.

28
00:02:16,622 --> 00:02:19,620
In the app router, you don't write a routes config file.

29
00:02:19,918 --> 00:02:24,196
The folder structure inside your app directory is your URL structure.

30
00:02:25,486 --> 00:02:29,860
A folder called blog with a page file inside becomes slash blog.

31
00:02:30,254 --> 00:02:38,276
Square brackets make a dynamic segment, so slash blog slash anything matches the slug folder, and you get the slug as a parameter.

32
00:02:39,566 --> 00:02:41,860
Only files named page are public.

33
00:02:42,222 --> 00:02:49,956
Everything else in that folder, like components or tests, stays private, so you can keep related code right next to the route.

34
00:02:51,278 --> 00:02:52,560
Then there are layouts.

35
00:02:52,910 --> 00:02:55,600
A layout file wraps every page below it.

36
00:02:55,854 --> 00:02:59,760
Your navbar and sidebar live in a layout, and here's the key part.

37
00:03:00,078 --> 00:03:03,428
When you navigate between pages, the layout stays mounted.

38
00:03:03,790 --> 00:03:06,212
It doesn't re-render, and its state is kept.

39
00:03:07,470 --> 00:03:09,280
There are a few more special files.

40
00:03:09,582 --> 00:03:12,080
Loading shows a fallback while the page loads.

41
00:03:12,462 --> 00:03:21,028
Error catches crashes in that part of the app, not found handles 404s, and route.ts lets you write API endpoints.

42
00:03:22,894 --> 00:03:26,500
This is the most important idea in modern Next.js.

43
00:03:26,894 --> 00:03:31,044
Every component inside the app folder is a server component by default.

44
00:03:32,302 --> 00:03:35,108
A server component runs only on the server.

45
00:03:35,502 --> 00:03:42,180
It can be a sync, it can read your database or file system directly, and its code is never sent to the browser.

46
00:03:42,606 --> 00:03:45,252
That's zero Java script for that component.

47
00:03:46,510 --> 00:03:48,836
But server components can't be interactive.

48
00:03:49,166 --> 00:03:52,772
No use state, no onClick, no browser APIs.

49
00:03:53,102 --> 00:03:56,196
For that, you add useClient at the very top of the file.

50
00:03:57,518 --> 00:03:59,396
UseClient marks a boundary.

51
00:03:59,726 --> 00:04:04,548
That component, and everything it imports, becomes part of the JavaScript bundle.

52
00:04:04,974 --> 00:04:09,200
Client components are still rendered to HTML on the server first.

53
00:04:09,518 --> 00:04:11,460
They just also run in the browser.

54
00:04:12,750 --> 00:04:22,148
So here's the rule of thumb Keep most of your tree on the server and push use client down to the small interactive leaves like buttons, forms, and menus.

55
00:04:22,606 --> 00:04:24,996
Less JavaScript, faster pages.

56
00:04:26,926 --> 00:04:28,432
Let's follow one request.

57
00:04:28,750 --> 00:04:30,992
You open slash blog slash hello.

58
00:04:31,438 --> 00:04:35,552
If your app has a proxy.ts file, the request reaches it first.

59
00:04:35,950 --> 00:04:38,756
In older versions, this file was called middleware.

60
00:04:40,046 --> 00:04:42,432
The proxy runs before anything renders.

61
00:04:42,766 --> 00:04:47,428
It's where you redirect logged-out users, rewrite URLs, or set headers.

62
00:04:47,790 --> 00:04:51,172
Keep it light because it runs on every matching request.

63
00:04:52,494 --> 00:04:55,524
Next, the router matches the URL to your folders.

64
00:04:55,886 --> 00:05:02,820
It finds the root layout, the blog layout, and the slug page, and renders them on the server as one component tree.

65
00:05:04,110 --> 00:05:05,952
Rendering produces two things.

66
00:05:06,222 --> 00:05:10,340
First, HTML, so the browser can paint the page immediately.

67
00:05:10,830 --> 00:05:13,028
Second, the RSC payload.

68
00:05:13,358 --> 00:05:21,284
That's a compact description of the rendered component tree with placeholders where client components go and links to their JavaScript files.

69
00:05:22,574 --> 00:05:25,632
In the browser, the HTML shows up first.

70
00:05:25,998 --> 00:05:33,892
Then the Java script for your client components downloads, and React uses the payload to attach event handlers to what's already on screen.

71
00:05:34,350 --> 00:05:36,356
This step is called hydration.

72
00:05:37,678 --> 00:05:41,124
So a user sees content before any Java script runs.

73
00:05:41,486 --> 00:05:46,724
And server components never hydrate at all because there's nothing in them to make interactive.

74
00:05:48,590 --> 00:05:50,832
Now, what if one part of your page is slow?

75
00:05:51,118 --> 00:05:53,348
Say the comments take three seconds to load.

76
00:05:53,678 --> 00:05:57,220
Without streaming, the whole page waits for the slowest piece.

77
00:05:58,510 --> 00:06:01,764
With suspense, you wrap the slow part and give it a fallback.

78
00:06:02,158 --> 00:06:06,980
Next, JS sends the rest of the page right away with the fallback sitting in place.

79
00:06:08,302 --> 00:06:15,076
When the comments finish on the server, their HTML is streamed down in the same response and swapped into place.

80
00:06:15,502 --> 00:06:18,596
No extra request and no client side fetching.

81
00:06:19,886 --> 00:06:23,716
A loading file is just a suspense boundary around the whole page.

82
00:06:24,110 --> 00:06:29,028
So you can stream at the route level with loading or at the component level with suspense.

83
00:06:30,958 --> 00:06:33,472
Next question: When does the rendering happen?

84
00:06:33,806 --> 00:06:38,340
Some pages are the same for everyone, like a blog post or a pricing page.

85
00:06:38,670 --> 00:06:42,660
Those can be rendered once at build time and served from a CDN.

86
00:06:43,054 --> 00:06:44,356
That's static rendering.

87
00:06:45,646 --> 00:06:47,712
Other pages depend on the request.

88
00:06:48,078 --> 00:06:52,964
They read cookies, headers, or search params, like a dashboard for a logged in user.

89
00:06:53,262 --> 00:06:55,152
Those render on every request.

90
00:06:55,502 --> 00:06:56,932
That's dynamic rendering.

91
00:06:58,222 --> 00:07:03,236
Recent versions of Next.js are dynamic by default, with no hidden caching.

92
00:07:03,694 --> 00:07:09,540
You decide what gets cached, explicitly, with the use cache directive on a function or a component.

93
00:07:10,830 --> 00:07:12,672
And you can mix both on one page.

94
00:07:13,006 --> 00:07:18,564
A cached, static shell is sent instantly, while dynamic parts stream in through suspense.

95
00:07:18,958 --> 00:07:21,540
Static where you can, dynamic where you must.

96
00:07:22,830 --> 00:07:26,244
When cache data changes, you don't need to rebuild the site.

97
00:07:26,606 --> 00:07:33,348
You can revalidate it on a timer or on demand right after a change, and the next visitor gets a fresh version.

98
00:07:33,806 --> 00:07:37,156
That idea is called incremental static regeneration.

99
00:07:39,054 --> 00:07:43,712
Once the first page has loaded, Next.js behaves like a single page app.

100
00:07:44,014 --> 00:07:47,396
When you click a link component, there's no full page reload.

101
00:07:48,654 --> 00:07:56,352
Instead, the router fetches only the RSC payload for the new route, and React updates just the part of the page that changed.

102
00:07:56,814 --> 00:08:00,804
Your shared layouts stay exactly as they are, including their state.

103
00:08:02,126 --> 00:08:03,232
And it gets faster.

104
00:08:03,662 --> 00:08:08,228
When a link scrolls into view, Next.js can prefetch that route in the background.

105
00:08:08,622 --> 00:08:13,412
So by the time you click, the loading shell or even the whole page is already there.

106
00:08:14,702 --> 00:08:21,316
Back and forward navigation feels instant too, because recently visited routes are kept in a client-side cache.

107
00:08:23,150 --> 00:08:24,992
So far we've only read data.

108
00:08:25,326 --> 00:08:27,552
What about writing it, like submitting a comment?

109
00:08:27,918 --> 00:08:29,952
NextJS has server actions.

110
00:08:30,382 --> 00:08:36,004
A function marked with use server runs on the server, but you can call it straight from your components.

111
00:08:37,294 --> 00:08:39,072
You pass it to a forms action.

112
00:08:39,406 --> 00:08:43,588
When the user submits, Next.js sends the request to the server for you.

113
00:08:43,982 --> 00:08:46,884
No API route to write and no fetch call.

114
00:08:48,142 --> 00:08:53,956
After the change, you tell Next.js what's now stale, for example with Revalidate Path.

115
00:08:54,382 --> 00:08:59,940
It re renders that route on the server and sends the updated UI back in the same response.

116
00:09:01,230 --> 00:09:10,532
And if you still need a classic API, say for a mobile app or a webhook, you write a route.ts file that handles get and post requests directly.

117
00:09:12,398 --> 00:09:13,668
Finally, shipping it.

118
00:09:14,062 --> 00:09:22,276
When you run next build, Turbopack compiles your code and splits it by route, so each page only loads the Java script it actually needs.

119
00:09:23,534 --> 00:09:30,020
The build also works out which routes can be pre-rendered as static files and which need a server at request time.

120
00:09:30,446 --> 00:09:34,468
You'll see that in the build output, as a little symbol next to each route.

121
00:09:35,758 --> 00:09:36,640
Then you deploy.

122
00:09:37,006 --> 00:09:38,948
On Vercel, it's zero config.

123
00:09:39,342 --> 00:09:47,300
But Next.js also runs on any node server in a Docker container or as a fully static export if you don't need a server at all.

124
00:09:48,622 --> 00:09:56,292
Static files and images go out to a CDN close to your users, and the server only does the work that truly needs a server.

125
00:09:58,158 --> 00:10:02,800
Before we wrap up, here are three questions interviewers love to ask about Next.js.

126
00:10:03,470 --> 00:10:06,884
Pause the video and try to answer each one before I do.

127
00:10:08,174 --> 00:10:12,000
1. What's the difference between a server component and a client component?

128
00:10:12,320 --> 00:10:16,708
Server components run only on the server and ship no JavaScript.

129
00:10:17,038 --> 00:10:19,492
Client components are marked with use client.

130
00:10:19,822 --> 00:10:23,684
They're rendered on the server too, and then they hydrate in the browser.

131
00:10:24,942 --> 00:10:26,720
2. What is hydration?

132
00:10:27,118 --> 00:10:36,100
It's React attaching event handlers to the HTML, the server already rendered, so the page becomes interactive without being rebuilt from scratch.

133
00:10:37,454 --> 00:10:40,800
3. When is a page static and when is it dynamic?

134
00:10:41,134 --> 00:10:44,676
If it doesn't depend on the request, it can be pre-rendered and cached.

135
00:10:45,006 --> 00:10:49,828
If it reads cookies, headers, or search params, it renders on every request.

136
00:10:51,662 --> 00:10:52,880
Let's put it all together.

137
00:10:53,166 --> 00:10:55,076
Your folders define the routes.

138
00:10:55,438 --> 00:11:01,348
A request passes through the proxy, the router matches it, and your server components render on the server.

139
00:11:02,670 --> 00:11:05,028
The browser gets HTML first.

140
00:11:05,358 --> 00:11:11,492
Then the payload and the Java script for client components arrive, and hydration makes the page interactive.

141
00:11:11,918 --> 00:11:14,372
Slow parts stream in through suspense.

142
00:11:15,662 --> 00:11:25,476
After that, links navigate without reloads, server actions handle your changes, and caching decides what gets rendered once, versus on every request.

143
00:11:26,766 --> 00:11:29,280
And that's how Next.js actually works.

144
00:11:29,646 --> 00:11:35,908
If this helped, save it for your next interview and follow frontendschool.in for more deep dives like this one.

